| **WireMock** | Mocking event stores and validating retry logic |
| **Prometheus + Grafana** | Monitoring backpressure metrics and connection health |
| **JMeter** | Alternative for complex correlation ID and header validation |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests reliability of composite APIs with dependent services.

## Key Concepts
- Downstream latency, timeouts, partial failures, circuit breakers
- Idempotent retries, exponential backoff, observability
- End-to-end timing and schema validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test using jest + nock
const nock = require('nock');
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../app');
test('partial data when one downstream times out', async () => {
  nock('http://sales').get('/daily').delay(3000).reply(200, {sales: 1000});
  nock('http://customers').get('/daily').reply(200, {customers: 50});
  const res = await request(app).get('/reports/daily');
  expect(res.statusCode).toBe(200);
  expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('sales');
  expect(res.headers).toHaveProperty('x-downstream-warn');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test circuit-breaker behavior under sustained latency?
- What metrics would you validate in your observability stack?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for idempotent POST semantics are common in production APIs to avoid duplicate side effects. This question assesses practical planning, edge-case coverage, and tooling familiarity.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent POST with Idempotency-Key usage
- Database state validation vs. idempotent responses
- Rate limiting and retry behavior
- Schema validation and header propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test idea (pseudo):
const payload = { itemId: 'A1', qty: 1, customerId: 'C42' };
const key = 'idemp-123';
const r1 = post('/orders', payload, {'X-Idempotency-Key': key});
const r2 = post('/orders', payload, {'X-Idempotency-Key': key});
assert(r1.orderId === r2.orderId);
assert(r1.status in [201,200]);
```

